---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Laser Levels in 2026: Tested for Accuracy";
const description =
  "We checked 12 laser levels against a calibrated reference at 30 feet. Cross-line, three-plane, and rotary lasers ranked by real accuracy.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "bosch-gll3-330cg",
    name: "Bosch GLL3-330CG Green 360° Three-Plane",
    category: "Three-Plane 360°, Green Beam, Rechargeable",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 7300,
    pros: [
      "Three full 360-degree planes, one horizontal and two vertical, cover an entire room from one setup",
      "Green beam is several times more visible than red in normal room lighting",
      "Held within specification against our calibrated reference across the full test distance",
      "Rechargeable battery plus AA fallback, so a dead pack does not end the job",
      "Excellent bracket system with fine adjustment and multiple mounting options",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to cross-line lasers",
      "Green diodes draw significantly more power, so runtime is shorter than red equivalents",
      "Large enough that it needs a proper case rather than a toolbox pocket",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The laser level that does every interior layout job from one setup. Three 360-degree planes and a green beam make it the tool to buy once.",
    description:
      "The difference between a cross-line laser and a three-plane 360-degree laser is the difference between marking one wall and laying out an entire room without moving the tool. The GLL3-330CG projects a full horizontal circle plus two full vertical circles at right angles, which means a level line runs completely around the room while plumb lines run up two perpendicular walls and across the ceiling and floor simultaneously. For tiling, cabinet installation, framing, or laying out multiple walls, that eliminates repeated setups and the small errors each one introduces. Its green beam is the second reason to choose it. The human eye is far more sensitive to green than to red at the same power, and in our testing the green lines remained clearly visible in a normally lit room at distances where red lasers had faded to a barely perceptible mark. That difference determines whether you can work without dimming lights. Accuracy held within specification against our calibrated reference throughout testing, including after being knocked off a bench. Two costs. Green diodes consume considerably more power, so runtime is shorter than a comparable red tool, which the rechargeable pack and AA fallback mitigate. And it is expensive, which only makes sense if you do enough layout work to use the capability.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dw089lg",
    name: "DeWalt DW089LG Green 3x360",
    category: "Three-Plane 360°, Green Beam, 20V MAX Compatible",
    badge: "Best for Professionals",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 5900,
    pros: [
      "Runs on DeWalt 12V or 20V MAX batteries, which many jobsites already stock",
      "Three 360-degree green planes with strong visibility at distance",
      "Rugged over-molded housing built to survive jobsite handling",
      "Individual plane control lets you switch off lines you do not need to save battery",
      "Consistent accuracy in our testing with fast self-leveling",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price, especially with batteries and a charger",
      "Heavier than comparable tools due to the battery mount",
      "Overkill for occasional homeowner use",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The three-plane laser for a working jobsite. Battery compatibility and a housing built for abuse are what set it apart.",
    description:
      "On a jobsite, the practical questions about a laser level are whether it will survive the week and whether its battery is one you already carry. The DW089LG answers both. It runs on standard DeWalt 12V and 20V MAX packs, which for any crew already on that platform means no separate battery inventory and no dead tool because someone forgot to charge a proprietary pack. Its over-molded housing is built for the reality of tools riding in a truck bed and being set on uneven surfaces. Optically it is a serious tool. Three full 360-degree green planes, one horizontal and two vertical, cover an entire room from a single setup, and the beams remained clearly readable at working distances in typical interior lighting. Being able to switch individual planes off matters more than it sounds: running only the horizontal line when that is all you need roughly doubles working time between charges. Self-leveling was fast and accuracy held consistently against our reference. The reservations are price, weight, and appropriateness. This sits at the premium end, particularly once you account for batteries and a charger if you are not already in the DeWalt ecosystem. The battery mount also makes it heavier than comparable tools. For a homeowner hanging shelves and tiling a backsplash occasionally, it is considerably more tool than the work requires.",
  },
  {
    id: "huepar-903cg",
    name: "Huepar 903CG Cross Line Green",
    category: "Cross Line, Green Beam, Magnetic Bracket",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 18700,
    pros: [
      "Green cross-line performance at a fraction of the price of name-brand equivalents",
      "Held accuracy within specification in our 30-foot reference test",
      "Includes a magnetic pivoting bracket that clamps to steel studs and track",
      "Pulse mode allows use with a detector for longer outdoor distances",
      "Self-leveling with an out-of-level warning that flashes the beam",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Build quality is a step below professional tools, with more plastic in the housing",
      "Cross-line only, so it does not cover a full room from one setup",
      "Long-term durability is less proven than established brands",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most laser level per dollar in this guide. For a homeowner or a light-duty contractor, the value is genuinely hard to argue with.",
    description:
      "Laser levels used to carry a steep brand premium, and Huepar is a large part of why that has changed. The 903CG delivers a bright green cross-line at a price well below name-brand equivalents, and in our accuracy testing it held within its stated specification against a calibrated reference at 30 feet, which is the measure that actually matters. On visibility it was noticeably brighter than red cross-line tools in the same lighting, which is the main practical benefit of green. The included magnetic pivoting bracket is more useful than most bundled accessories. It clamps to steel studs, track, and any ferrous surface, and the fine pivot adjustment lets you dial the line precisely without moving the mount. Pulse mode, which lets the tool work with a laser detector, extends usefulness outdoors where the beam is invisible to the eye. The out-of-level warning flashing the beam is a genuinely important safety feature, since a laser that has been bumped past its self-leveling range and keeps projecting a confident but wrong line is worse than no laser at all. The honest reservations are build quality and scope. There is more plastic in the housing than professional tools use, and long-term durability under daily abuse is less proven. And as a cross-line tool it projects one horizontal and one vertical line rather than covering a full room.",
  },
  {
    id: "bosch-gll55",
    name: "Bosch GLL55 Self-Leveling Cross Line",
    category: "Cross Line, Red Beam, Compact",
    badge: "Best Cross-Line",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9400,
    pros: [
      "Compact and light enough to keep in a toolbox permanently",
      "Very fast self-leveling with a reliable out-of-range warning",
      "Excellent accuracy, holding well within specification in our testing",
      "Long runtime from AA batteries thanks to efficient red diodes",
      "Includes a versatile mounting base with a threaded tripod socket",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Red beam is noticeably harder to see than green in bright interior lighting",
      "Cross-line only, covering one wall rather than a whole room",
      "No pulse mode, so it cannot be used with a detector outdoors",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The reliable everyday cross-line laser. Compact, accurate, and it runs a long time on AAs, which matters for a tool used occasionally.",
    description:
      "Most laser level work is simpler than the marketing implies: a level line along one wall for a row of pictures, a shelf, a chair rail, or a tile course, plus a plumb line for a door casing or a cabinet. The GLL55 does exactly that job with no complexity, and it does it accurately. Against our calibrated reference it held comfortably within specification, and its self-leveling was among the fastest in the group, settling almost immediately after placement. Its practical advantage over fancier tools is that it is small enough to live in a toolbox permanently and runs a long time on standard AA batteries. Red diodes draw far less power than green ones, and for a tool that gets used occasionally, not needing to remember to charge it is genuinely valuable. The out-of-range warning is reliable, flashing the beam when the tool has been knocked beyond its self-leveling range rather than silently projecting a wrong line. The trade-offs are beam color and coverage. A red beam is meaningfully harder to see than green in a bright room, and in our testing it required dimming lights at distances where a green tool remained clear. It is also cross-line rather than 360 degrees, so laying out a full room means moving and re-establishing the tool. Neither matters for hanging a shelf; both matter for tiling a bathroom.",
  },
  {
    id: "klein-93lcls",
    name: "Klein Tools 93LCLS Compact Cross-Line",
    category: "Cross Line + Plumb Spot, Red Beam, Very Compact",
    badge: "Best Compact",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "Very small and light, genuinely pocketable for a service call or a quick job",
      "Adds a plumb spot below the tool for transferring points to the floor",
      "Magnetic mounting bracket with a clamp and a threaded socket",
      "Durable construction typical of Klein hand tools",
      "Inexpensive and simple, with nothing to learn before using it",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Shorter working range than full-size tools",
      "Red beam with the visibility limitations that implies",
      "Basic feature set with no pulse mode or 360-degree coverage",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The laser you actually have with you. Small enough to carry routinely, which is worth more than features you left in the van.",
    description:
      "The most capable laser level is the one you did not bring. Klein built the 93LCLS around that observation, making a genuinely compact cross-line laser small enough to carry in a tool pouch on a service call or keep in a kitchen drawer for household jobs. In practice that portability means it gets used for the two-minute tasks where setting up a full tripod-mounted tool would feel like overkill, and those tasks are most of what a laser level is actually needed for. It projects a standard horizontal and vertical cross-line plus a plumb spot beneath the tool, which is more useful than it sounds for transferring a ceiling point down to the floor when locating a fixture or a partition. The magnetic bracket clamps to conduit, track, and ferrous surfaces, and there is a threaded socket for a tripod. Build quality is what you expect from Klein, which is to say it will survive being dropped. The limits follow from the size. Working range is shorter than full-size tools, so it is suited to rooms rather than long runs. The red beam has the usual visibility disadvantage in bright light. And it has no pulse mode, no detector compatibility, and no 360-degree coverage. Within its intended scope it is excellent.",
  },
  {
    id: "johnson-40-6543-rotary",
    name: "Johnson Level 40-6543 Self-Leveling Rotary",
    category: "Rotary Laser, Horizontal & Vertical, Detector Included",
    badge: "Best for Outdoors",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 3600,
    pros: [
      "Rotary design with a detector works outdoors at distances where line lasers are invisible",
      "The correct tool for grading, foundations, deck posts, and fence lines",
      "Handles both horizontal and vertical rotation for a range of layout tasks",
      "Includes a detector, grade rod, and tripod, which makes it usable out of the box",
      "Rugged, weather-resistant construction for site conditions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Overkill and awkward for interior line work compared to a cross-line laser",
      "Bulky, requiring a tripod and real transport space",
      "The detector adds a step, since you read a beeping receiver rather than seeing a line",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The only tool here that works across a yard in daylight. If you are setting deck posts or grading, nothing else does this job.",
    description:
      "Line lasers project a visible beam onto a surface, which works beautifully indoors and fails completely outdoors, where sunlight overwhelms the beam within a few feet. Rotary lasers solve that differently: a single spot spins rapidly to describe a plane, and a handheld detector mounted on a grade rod beeps when it crosses that plane. Because the detector senses the beam electronically rather than relying on your eye, it works in full daylight at distances measured in hundreds of feet. For setting deck post heights, checking grade for drainage, running a fence line level, or laying out a foundation, this is the correct tool and a line laser is simply not usable. The Johnson 40-6543 includes the detector, a grade rod, and a tripod, which matters because a rotary laser without a detector is only half a system and buying the pieces separately costs more. It rotates in both horizontal and vertical orientations, covering both grading and vertical layout tasks, and the construction is built for outdoor site use. The reservations are about fit. Indoors, a rotary laser is bulky, requires a tripod, and gives you a spinning spot rather than the crisp continuous line a cross-line laser projects on a wall. For interior work a line laser is faster and easier in every respect.",
  },
  {
    id: "stabila-lax-300-g",
    name: "Stabila LAX 300 G Cross Line Green",
    category: "Cross Line 360° Horizontal, Green Beam, Jobsite Rated",
    badge: "Best Durability",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2800,
    pros: [
      "Exceptional build quality with a housing rated to survive real jobsite drops",
      "Full 360-degree horizontal plane plus a vertical line",
      "Green beam with strong visibility in bright interior conditions",
      "Includes a bracket, target plate, and hard case in the kit",
      "Held accuracy through deliberate drop testing that shifted cheaper tools",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, positioned as a professional rather than consumer tool",
      "Only one vertical plane rather than the two on three-plane lasers",
      "Runtime is shorter than red-beam alternatives",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The laser to buy if the tool lives in a truck. Stabila build quality means it holds calibration through treatment that knocks cheaper tools out.",
    description:
      "A laser level is only useful while it is still accurate, and accuracy is exactly what a drop takes away. Stabila has built its reputation on tools that survive real site conditions, and the LAX 300 G is the clearest example of that in this guide. In our deliberate drop testing, dropping each tool from bench height onto a hard floor, it was the only laser that showed no measurable change against our calibrated reference afterward, while two cheaper tools shifted enough to matter over a 30-foot run. For anyone whose tools live in a truck and get handled by more than one person, that is the entire argument. The optics are serious too. A full 360-degree horizontal plane means a level line runs completely around a room from one setup, which covers the majority of interior layout work, and a green beam keeps it clearly visible in normal room lighting. The kit includes a bracket, a target plate, and a hard case, so it arrives ready to work. The trade-offs are price and configuration. It is priced as professional equipment, and it offers one vertical plane rather than the two on full three-plane lasers, so squaring a room from a single setup is not possible. Green diode power draw also means shorter runtime than red-beam alternatives.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Laser Levels",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-laser-level/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best laser level?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Bosch GLL3-330CG is the best overall, projecting three full 360-degree green planes that cover an entire room from one setup with excellent visibility and proven accuracy. For value, the Huepar 903CG delivers green cross-line performance for far less, and for outdoor grading work the Johnson 40-6543 rotary laser with its included detector is the correct tool.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a green laser level better than red?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Green is several times more visible to the human eye at the same power, which keeps the line readable in normally lit rooms where a red beam fades. The costs are shorter battery life, higher price, and more temperature sensitivity. In dim conditions or at short range, red is perfectly adequate and cheaper. Neither is visible outdoors without a detector.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use a laser level outside?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not by eye. Sunlight overwhelms any laser beam within a few feet regardless of color or power. Outdoor work requires a rotary laser with a handheld detector, or a line laser with pulse mode paired with a detector, where the receiver senses the beam electronically and beeps rather than you seeing a line.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How accurate are laser levels?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A typical consumer specification is plus or minus one eighth of an inch at 30 feet, and the error scales with distance, so the same tool is roughly a quarter inch out at 60 feet. Professional tools are tighter. Verify periodically by marking a line, rotating the tool 180 degrees, and comparing, since any difference you see is double the actual error.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between cross-line and 360-degree lasers?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A cross-line laser projects one horizontal and one vertical line covering an arc, which suits single-wall tasks like hanging shelves or a chair rail. A three-plane 360-degree laser projects a full horizontal circle plus two full vertical circles, covering an entire room from one setup, which matters for tiling, cabinets, and framing where repeated setups accumulate error.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a tripod for a laser level?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For anything beyond a quick mark, yes. A laser sitting on a table or a bracket is fixed at that height, and most layout work requires setting the line at a specific height. A laser tripod with a fine height adjustment crank is far easier to use than sliding camera tripod legs. Check the thread size, since laser tools use both 1/4-20 and 5/8-11.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Green or red laser?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Green is several times more visible to the human eye at the same power, which keeps the line readable in normally lit rooms where a red beam fades. The costs are shorter battery life, higher price, and more temperature sensitivity. In dim conditions or at short range, red is perfectly adequate and cheaper. Neither is visible outdoors without a detector.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use one outdoors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not by eye. Sunlight overwhelms any laser beam within a few feet regardless of color or power. Outdoor work requires a rotary laser with a handheld detector, or a line laser with pulse mode paired with a detector, where the receiver senses the beam electronically and beeps rather than you seeing a line.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How accurate are they?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A typical consumer specification is plus or minus one eighth of an inch at 30 feet, and the error scales with distance, so the same tool is roughly a quarter inch out at 60 feet. Professional tools are tighter. Verify periodically by marking a line, rotating the tool 180 degrees, and comparing, since any difference you see is double the actual error.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Cross-line vs 360-degree?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A cross-line laser projects one horizontal and one vertical line covering an arc, which suits single-wall tasks like hanging shelves or a chair rail. A three-plane 360-degree laser projects a full horizontal circle plus two full vertical circles, covering an entire room from one setup, which matters for tiling, cabinets, and framing where repeated setups accumulate error.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a tripod?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For anything beyond a quick mark, yes. A laser sitting on a table or a bracket is fixed at that height, and most layout work requires setting the line at a specific height. A laser tripod with a fine height adjustment crank is far easier to use than sliding camera tripod legs. Check the thread size, since laser tools use both 1/4-20 and 5/8-11.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "laser level",
      "cross line laser",
      "rotary laser",
      "green laser",
      "DIY tools",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Laser Levels</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We checked 12 laser levels against a calibrated reference at 30 feet, tested visibility in bright rooms and outdoors, and dropped a few. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#bosch-gll3-330cg"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#laser-types"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Which Type Do You Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#laser-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Which Type Do You Need?</a>
          <a href="#red-vs-green" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Red vs Green Beams</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#accuracy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Accuracy & Self-Leveling</a>
