        <!-- SECTION 1: Line, Cross-Line, Three-Plane, and Rotary -->
        <div id="laser-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Line, Cross-Line, Three-Plane, and Rotary
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laser levels differ mainly in what geometry they project, and that determines which jobs they can do in one setup. Buying the wrong type is the most common and most frustrating mistake in this category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cross-Line Lasers</h3>

          <p>
            Project one horizontal and one vertical line that cross, typically covering an arc rather than a full circle. This is the right tool for hanging pictures and shelves, running a chair rail, aligning a tile course on one wall, or setting a door casing plumb. They are compact, inexpensive, and cover the large majority of household layout tasks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-Plane 360-Degree Lasers</h3>

          <p>
            Project a full horizontal circle plus two full vertical circles at right angles. A level line runs entirely around the room and plumb lines run up two perpendicular walls, across the ceiling, and down to the floor, all from one setup. This is what you want for tiling a whole bathroom, installing cabinets, framing, or any job spanning multiple walls, because it removes the accumulated error of repeated setups.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotary Lasers</h3>

          <p>
            Spin a single spot rapidly to describe a plane, and are read with a handheld detector rather than by eye. Because the detector senses the beam electronically, rotary lasers work outdoors in full sun at long range, which no line laser can do. They are the correct tool for grading, deck posts, fence lines, and foundations, and clumsy for interior line work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Point and Dot Lasers</h3>

          <p>
            Project individual reference dots rather than lines, typically plumb up and down plus level points. Used mainly for transferring points between floor and ceiling, such as locating a light fixture directly above a floor mark. A niche tool, and several cross-line lasers include a plumb spot that covers the common case.
          </p>

