        <!-- SECTION 2: Red vs Green Beams -->
        <div id="red-vs-green" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Red vs Green Beams
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beam color is not cosmetic. It changes how visible the line is, how long the battery lasts, and how much the tool costs, and the right choice depends on where you work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Green Looks Brighter</h3>

          <p>
            The human eye is most sensitive to wavelengths in the green part of the spectrum, and at equal output power a green laser line appears several times brighter than a red one. That is a property of your eye rather than of the laser, and it is why green tools remain readable in a normally lit room at distances where a red line has faded to almost nothing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Green Costs</h3>

          <p>
            Green laser diodes are more complex and consume considerably more power, which means shorter runtime from the same battery and a higher purchase price. Green tools also tend to be more temperature sensitive, and some have a brief warm-up before reaching full brightness in cold conditions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Red Is the Right Choice</h3>

          <p>
            In dim conditions, at short range, or where you can control the lighting, a red laser is perfectly adequate and gives you longer runtime and a lower price. For a tool used occasionally around a house, red plus AA batteries means it works whenever you pick it up rather than needing a charge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Neither Works Outdoors</h3>

          <p>
            In daylight, both red and green beams become invisible within a few feet. Outdoor work requires a laser with pulse mode and a handheld detector that senses the beam electronically, or a rotary laser system. Beam color makes no meaningful difference to this limitation.
          </p>

