        <!-- SECTION 4: Accuracy, Self-Leveling, and What the Specs Mean -->
        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Accuracy, Self-Leveling, and What the Specs Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Accuracy specifications look similar across the category and mean different things depending on distance, and self-leveling has a limit that catches people out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Accuracy is expressed per distance.</strong> A typical consumer specification is plus or minus one eighth of an inch at 30 feet. The error scales, so the same tool at 60 feet is roughly a quarter inch out. For hanging a shelf that is irrelevant; for running a tile course the length of a room it matters, and for outdoor grading over 100 feet it matters a great deal.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Self-leveling has a working range.</strong> Most self-leveling lasers compensate for roughly three to four degrees of tilt using an internal pendulum. Set the tool on a surface outside that range and it cannot level itself. Good tools flash the beam or refuse to project when out of range; cheap ones may simply project a confident, wrong line, which is far more dangerous than no line at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the out-of-range warning before you trust the tool.</strong> Tilt it deliberately past the self-leveling range and confirm that it warns you. Knowing that the tool will tell you when it cannot level is the difference between a reference you can rely on and one you have to second-guess.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Manual mode disables leveling deliberately.</strong> Most tools offer a locked mode that projects a line at whatever angle the tool sits, which is what you want for marking a stair stringer or a deliberate slope. It is also a trap: leaving the tool in manual mode and assuming the line is level produces confidently wrong work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Verify calibration periodically.</strong> Set the laser 30 feet from a wall, mark the line, rotate the tool 180 degrees, reposition it to project onto the same spot, and compare. Any difference is twice the actual error. Doing this after a drop, and once a year regardless, catches a tool that has quietly drifted.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Check That Takes Two Minutes</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Place the laser roughly 30 feet from a wall and mark where the horizontal line falls. Move the tool to about 5 feet from the same wall, adjust height until the line hits the same mark, then turn the tool 180 degrees to project on the opposite wall and mark it. Move back to the far position and compare. If the marks disagree, the error you see is double the tool's actual error, and it needs recalibration.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Mounts, Tripods, and Detectors -->
        <div id="mounts-and-accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Mounts, Tripods, and Detectors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The laser projects the line, but how you position and hold it determines whether the line lands where you need it. Mounting is where most of the practical frustration in this category lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tripods with fine height adjustment.</strong> A standard camera tripod works with most lasers through the common threaded socket, but a laser tripod with a fine height adjustment crank is far better, because setting a line to an exact height by loosening and sliding a tripod leg is genuinely difficult. Look for the thread size, since laser tools use both 1/4-20 and 5/8-11 depending on class.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Magnetic pivoting brackets.</strong> These clamp to steel studs, track, conduit, and any ferrous surface, and the pivot lets you fine-tune the line without moving the mount. For interior work in commercial construction, this is often more useful than a tripod. Many tools include one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pole mounts and grabber poles.</strong> A telescoping pole braced between floor and ceiling holds a laser at any height without floor space, which is invaluable in a room where a tripod would be in the way. Worth having if you do ceiling work or tiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Detectors are mandatory outdoors.</strong> A handheld receiver senses the beam electronically and beeps when it crosses the plane, which is the only way to use a laser in daylight. The laser must support pulse mode for a detector to work, so check that specification if outdoor use is on your list.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Target plates improve visibility.</strong> A small red or green plastic plate held at the far end of a run makes a faint line dramatically easier to see, which extends the practical working range of a red laser considerably. They cost very little and are the cheapest way to improve a tool you already own.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling and wall brackets with a scale.</strong> Some brackets include a graduated scale that lets you offset the line a precise amount from a reference, which is useful for tasks like setting a suspended ceiling grid below a known point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Indoor Versus Outdoor Use -->
        <div id="indoor-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Indoor Versus Outdoor Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most common disappointment with laser levels is buying one for outdoor work and discovering the beam vanishes in daylight. The two environments genuinely require different tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            A line laser projects a visible line onto walls, floors, and ceilings, and you read it directly with your eye. Green beams stay readable in normally lit rooms, red beams often need lights dimmed at distance. Cross-line lasers cover single-wall tasks, and three-plane 360-degree lasers cover whole rooms from one setup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoors</h3>

          <p>
            Sunlight overwhelms any laser beam within a few feet, so the line is simply not visible. Outdoor work requires either a rotary laser with a detector, or a line laser with pulse mode paired with a detector, in both cases reading the beam by an audible signal from a receiver rather than by eye. Beam color is irrelevant to this.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Consequence</h3>

          <p>
            If your list of jobs includes setting deck posts, checking drainage grade, running a fence line, or laying out a patio, you need a detector-compatible system. If it is all interior work, a line laser without pulse mode is fine and cheaper. If it is both, buy a line laser with pulse mode and add a detector, which is the most flexible arrangement short of owning two tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twilight Is Not a Workaround</h3>

          <p>
            People sometimes plan to do outdoor laser work at dusk to make the beam visible. It works for short distances and is a poor plan for a real job, since the usable window is brief and you are working in fading light on tasks that benefit from seeing clearly. Buy the detector.
          </p>
        </div>

