              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Green or red laser?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Green is several times more visible to the human eye at the same power, which keeps the line readable in normally lit rooms where a red beam fades. The costs are shorter battery life, higher price, and more temperature sensitivity. In dim conditions or at short range, red is perfectly adequate and cheaper. Neither is visible outdoors without a detector.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use one outdoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not by eye. Sunlight overwhelms any laser beam within a few feet regardless of color or power. Outdoor work requires a rotary laser with a handheld detector, or a line laser with pulse mode paired with a detector, where the receiver senses the beam electronically and beeps rather than you seeing a line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How accurate are they?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical consumer specification is plus or minus one eighth of an inch at 30 feet, and the error scales with distance, so the same tool is roughly a quarter inch out at 60 feet. Professional tools are tighter. Verify periodically by marking a line, rotating the tool 180 degrees, and comparing, since any difference you see is double the actual error.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Cross-line vs 360-degree?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A cross-line laser projects one horizontal and one vertical line covering an arc, which suits single-wall tasks like hanging shelves or a chair rail. A three-plane 360-degree laser projects a full horizontal circle plus two full vertical circles, covering an entire room from one setup, which matters for tiling, cabinets, and framing where repeated setups accumulate error.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a tripod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anything beyond a quick mark, yes. A laser sitting on a table or a bracket is fixed at that height, and most layout work requires setting the line at a specific height. A laser tripod with a fine height adjustment crank is far easier to use than sliding camera tripod legs. Check the thread size, since laser tools use both 1/4-20 and 5/8-11.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Laser Level Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laser level problems are rarely about the tool being faulty and almost always about the type, the mode, or an unverified assumption.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Line Laser for Outdoor Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The beam simply is not visible in daylight, and no amount of brightness or beam color changes that. Outdoor grading, deck posts, and fence lines require a rotary laser or a pulse-mode line laser paired with a handheld detector. This is the single most common disappointment in the category.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Tool in Manual Mode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manual mode disables self-leveling so you can project a deliberate slope, which is genuinely useful for stair stringers and drainage. It is also a trap: forget to switch back and the tool projects a confident line at whatever angle it happens to sit, and the resulting work is precisely, consistently wrong.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Checking Calibration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laser levels drift, and a drop can shift one enough to matter without any visible damage. The two-minute check of marking a line, rotating the tool 180 degrees, and comparing catches it. Doing this after any significant knock, and once a year regardless, is the difference between a reference and a guess.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Cross-Line When the Job Needs 360 Degrees</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
